Compile hints: compile with its script a function called the last time
the script ran.

Patch 0021 compiles a nested function at its first call, which parses
it twice: with its script, to find what it captures, and again when it
is called. A page that runs the same scripts again (a reload, a later
visit) calls mostly the same functions. With JS_SetCompileHints the
embedder supplies two hooks:

- 'hints', called before the runtime compiles a script, a module or the
  code given to eval or Function (not a direct eval) of min_len bytes or
  more that keeps its text, with that text. It returns a nonzero id for
  the script (0: none) and may return the offsets in the text of the
  functions to compile with it, sorted. js_fd_lazy_ok keeps a function
  at one of those offsets compiled with its script.
- 'compiled', called after a function of a script with an id is
  compiled at its first call, with the id, the script's length and the
  function's offset in the script.

A script's texts (JSSourceText, patch 0008) carry the id, the script's
length and where in the script the text starts (hint_id, hint_len,
hint_base): a script mostly made of functions has one text, but each
outermost function of any other script has its own, so the offset in
the script is hint_base plus the function's offset in its text. The
hooks' state is saved and restored around each compile, so an eval run
while a script is compiled, or a compile that fails, leaves the
enclosing one's alone.

Measured on the host, a page loaded and then reloaded in one run with
the hints kept in memory, against the same reload without hints
(2026-11-18): GitHub's repository page compiled 4 functions at their
first call on the reload instead of 1,065, and its script took 707 to
722 ms instead of 741 to 848 ms; Wikipedia's "PowerPC" article 83
instead of 647, 79 to 82 ms instead of 96 to 97 ms; Reddit 257 instead
of about 2,180. The script heap was 100 to 200 KB smaller.

